/// Metric (and some inch) screws, nuts, washers and dowel pins at catalog
/// nominal, with the clearance, counterbore, countersink or tapped hole each
/// needs.

// Fastener Library (fastener)
//
// Standard metric fasteners at true catalog nominal, and the holes that
// receive them. Sizes are the NOMINAL THREAD DIAMETER in millimeters
// (3.0 = M3), looked up in ISO tables covering M1.6-M12: ISO 4762 socket head
// cap screws, ISO 10642 countersunk screws, ISO 4032 hex nuts, ISO 7089
// washers, ISO 2338 dowel pins, and each screw's hole — ISO 273 clearance, its
// counterbore or countersink, or the tapped hole — as a drill.hole in the
// screw's own frame. See Holes.
//
// Conventions: units are millimeters; every part's axis is +Z with its
// seating plane at z = 0 — a screw's head sits above z = 0 and its shank
// hangs below, a nut and washer rest on z = 0, a dowel is centered. Place
// parts with remap().
//
// SIZES COME FROM mechanical/catalog and threads from mechanical/thread. The
// catalog holds one row per standard size carrying the pitch and every
// dimension below it, so a thread and the screw cut with it are the same row. A screw here and the hole bore taps for it are the
// same curve read from opposite sides, which is the only way a fit between
// them means anything. shcsThreaded carries the real form; shcsThreadedSin
// carries the cheap one; thread.stripe paints a thread on a smooth shank for
// nothing at all.
//
// Prove against the smooth forms; display the threaded ones. The threaded
// surface carries a fine helix, so interval proofs against it subdivide to
// thread scale for no engineering information the smooth nominal does not
// already prove.
//
// Or display the smooth form itself. thread.maleRelief is the thread as a
// displacement height on the screw's material, which only the renderer reads,
// so one object is both the form a proof runs against and the form on screen:
//
//   screw = fastener.shcs(6.0, 20.0);
//   steelMat, steelColor = metal.steel();
//   screw.material = material.withDisplacement(steelMat,
//                        thread.maleRelief(6.0, 1.0, 0.0, -20.0));
//
// The relief stops at zTop, so pass the plane the thread stops under the head:
// z = 0 for a cap screw, the bottom of the cone for a countersunk one. See the
// Relief note in mechanical/thread.
//
// Doc convention follows builtin/base.gsl: `///` lines are public docs.

// =========================================================
// Sizes
// =========================================================

// There are no size tables here any more. Every dimension comes from one row
// in mechanical/catalog, shared with the thread pitch for that same size, so
// nothing can disagree about which sizes exist.
//
// That disagreement was real: thread carried pitches for M16, M20 and M24
// while every table here stopped at M12, so fastener.shcs(16.0, 30.0) built a
// screw with a zero head on a zero shank and said nothing about it. A size the
// catalog has no row for still returns 0, and so does a size whose row has a
// zero in that column — there is no ISO 4762 screw in M18, and asking for one
// gets you nothing built rather than something invented.

// The shank thread used to be a private sinusoid here, with its own root
// depth and its own phase. It is thread.male now — the ISO 68-1 form, at the
// depth the standard gives, on the datum the thread package publishes. The
// old one put its crest at z = 0 on +x and so does this, so a screw keeps the
// phase it has always had.

// pentXY: regular pentagon cross-section in XY, given width across flats —
// which for an odd-sided polygon is the APOTHEM doubled, centre to flat, not
// flat to flat: a pentagon has no opposite face to measure to.
//
// Five sides have no mirror pair, so there is no abs() shortcut here the way
// there is for four or six. It is five half-planes written out, at 72 degrees
// apart with one flat facing +x, and a max of half-planes is unit-gradient
// however many of them there are.
pentXY(acrossFlats) {
  h = acrossFlats / 2.0;
  return max(p.x - h,
             0.309017 * p.x + 0.951057 * p.y - h,
             -0.809017 * p.x + 0.587785 * p.y - h,
             -0.809017 * p.x - 0.587785 * p.y - h,
             0.309017 * p.x - 0.951057 * p.y - h);
}

// =========================================================
// A Screw is a Head, a Shank, a Drive and an End
// =========================================================
//
// THE STANDARDS SIT ON TOP OF THESE, they are not built into them. A head
// takes its own dimensions and nothing else — no nominal size, no table, no
// standard — so an ISO 4762 cap screw and one you invent are the same call with
// different numbers. fastener.shcs below is four lines of catalog lookup and
// one call to screw(); that is all a standard should ever be.
//
// It is also the only way the surface stays finite. Head style times drive
// times thread form times placement is a product, and naming every point of it
// is how this library ended up with shcs, shcsThreaded, shcsThreadedSin,
// countersunk and countersunkThreaded — five functions that differ in one term
// each and re-derive the same head and shank between them.
//
// EVERYTHING SHARES ONE DATUM: the seating plane is z = 0, the head is above
// it and the shank hangs below. A countersunk head is the exception that
// proves it — it seats flush, so its cone is below z = 0 and the plane still
// means the same thing, the face the screw pulls against.
//
// These are VALUES, not feature closures. A hole's features stack along an
// axis and fold, so drill hands out closures; a screw is one head, one shank
// and one drive, composed once, so plain fields are enough and none of the
// higher-order machinery is needed.

// =========================================================
// Heads
// =========================================================

/// A CYLINDRICAL head `dk` across and `k` tall with a chamfered top edge —
/// the socket head cap screw's head, ISO 4762's shape.
cylindricalHead(dk, k) {
  return cylindricalHead(dk, k, 0.2);
}

/// A cylindrical head with the top edge chamfer leg given.
cylindricalHead(dk, k, edge) {
  head = max(length(p.xy) - dk / 2.0, -p.z, p.z - k,
             ((length(p.xy) - dk / 2.0) + (p.z - k) + edge) * 0.7071);
  head.box = [[-dk / 2.0, -dk / 2.0, 0.0], [dk / 2.0, dk / 2.0, k]];
  return head;
}

/// A COUNTERSUNK head: a cone `dk` across at the seating plane narrowing to
/// `dSmall` where it meets the shank, at the given `included` angle in
/// radians. It sits BELOW z = 0, since a flat head seats flush.
///
/// The depth follows from the angle rather than being given: a 90-degree head
/// loses a millimetre of radius per millimetre of depth, which is why ISO
/// 10642's head depth is exactly (dk - d)/2.
countersunkHead(dk, dSmall, included) {
  half = included / 2.0;
  slope = tan(half);
  depth = (dk - dSmall) / (2.0 * slope);
  head = max((length(p.xy) - (dk / 2.0 + p.z * slope)) * cos(half),
             -depth - p.z, p.z);
  head.box = [[-dk / 2.0, -dk / 2.0, -depth], [dk / 2.0, dk / 2.0, 0.0]];
  return head;
}

/// A 90-degree countersunk head, the angle every ISO flat head uses.
countersunkHead(dk, dSmall) {
  return countersunkHead(dk, dSmall, 1.5707963);
}

/// A BUTTON head: the spherical cap `dk` across and `k` tall, ISO 7380's
/// shape. The crown radius follows from the two, since a cap is determined by
/// its span and its height.
buttonHead(dk, k) {
  crownR = (dk * dk / 4.0 + k * k) / (2.0 * k);
  // The cap is widest at the seating plane, dk across, while the sphere's
  // centre is below it; a head taller than it is wide takes in the equator.
  below = max(crownR - k, 0.0);
  rim = sqrt(crownR * crownR - below * below);
  head = max(length([p.x, p.y, p.z - (k - crownR)]) - crownR, -p.z);
  head.box = [[-rim, -rim, 0.0], [rim, rim, k]];
  return head;
}

/// A PAN head: a cylinder `dk` across and `k` tall whose top is domed to
/// `crownR`. ISO 7045's shape, and the everyday cross-recessed machine screw.
panHead(dk, k, crownR) {
  head = max(length(p.xy) - dk / 2.0, -p.z,
             length([p.x, p.y, p.z - (k - crownR)]) - crownR);
  head.box = [[-dk / 2.0, -dk / 2.0, 0.0], [dk / 2.0, dk / 2.0, k]];
  return head;
}

/// A pan head with the crown radius at 0.9 dk, near the usual proportion.
panHead(dk, k) {
  return panHead(dk, k, 0.9 * dk);
}

/// A HEX head `acrossFlats` wide and `k` tall, corners chamfered on the top
/// face the way a bolt head or a nut is. ISO 4014 and 4017's shape.
hexHead(acrossFlats, k) {
  // Flats on x, corners on y, 1/sqrt(3) of the width across flats out.
  corner = acrossFlats * 0.57735027;
  head = max(drill.hexXY(acrossFlats), -p.z, p.z - k,
             ((length(p.xy) - 0.52 * acrossFlats) + (p.z - k)) * 0.7071);
  head.box = [[-acrossFlats / 2.0, -corner, 0.0], [acrossFlats / 2.0, corner, k]];
  return head;
}

/// A SQUARE head `acrossFlats` wide and `k` tall, corners chamfered on the top
/// face. The oldest machine-made bolt head there is, and still what a set screw
/// on a shaft collar, a plough bolt or a track bolt carries: four flats take a
/// bigger wrench flat than six do at the same size, and an open-end wrench only
/// has to be re-seated every quarter turn.
squareHead(acrossFlats, k) {
  head = max(drill.squareXY(acrossFlats), -p.z, p.z - k,
             ((length(p.xy) - 0.55 * acrossFlats) + (p.z - k)) * 0.7071);
  head.box = [[-acrossFlats / 2.0, -acrossFlats / 2.0, 0.0],
              [acrossFlats / 2.0, acrossFlats / 2.0, k]];
  return head;
}

/// A PENTAGON head `acrossFlats` wide (centre to flat, doubled) and `k` tall.
///
/// Five sides is a SECURITY head: no standard wrench has five flats, so the
/// fastener needs its own tool, which is why it holds fire hydrants, water
/// meters, utility cabinets and electrical enclosures shut. Odd-sided also
/// means no pair of opposite faces for an adjustable wrench or a pair of pliers
/// to grip, which is the real reason it works and the reason it is measured
/// centre to flat.
pentHead(acrossFlats, k) {
  head = max(pentXY(acrossFlats), -p.z, p.z - k,
             ((length(p.xy) - 0.53 * acrossFlats) + (p.z - k)) * 0.7071);
  // A flat on +x, so a corner on -x, 1/cos(36 degrees) of the apothem out,
  // and the corners at 108 degrees reach furthest in y.
  head.box = [[-0.618034 * acrossFlats, -0.587785 * acrossFlats, 0.0],
              [acrossFlats / 2.0, 0.587785 * acrossFlats, k]];
  return head;
}

/// A FLANGED head: the head `dk` across and `k` tall standing on an integral
/// flange `dc` across and `c` thick, its underside chamfered. The flange
/// spreads the load the way a washer does, without the washer.
flangeHead(dk, k, dc, c) {
  // The crown runs halfway down into the flange rather than stopping on its
  // top: two solids bounded at one plane from either side meet in a sheet the
  // field touches zero on inside the metal. See Shanks.
  crown = max(length(p.xy) - dk / 2.0, c / 2.0 - p.z, p.z - k);
  flange = max(length(p.xy) - dc / 2.0, -p.z, p.z - c,
               ((length(p.xy) - dc / 2.0) + (p.z - c) + 0.1 * dc) * 0.7071);
  half = max(dk, dc) / 2.0;
  head = min(crown, flange);
  head.box = [[-half, -half, 0.0], [half, half, max(k, c)]];
  return head;
}

/// NO head at all — the set screw and the headless shoulder screw. It is the
/// empty solid, so screw() unions nothing and the shank stands alone.
noHead() {
  return nothing;
}

// =========================================================
// Head Marks
// =========================================================
//
// A Phillips and a Pozidriv recess are near enough identical to look at, and
// driving one with the other's bit is what rounds the recess out. So the HEAD
// is marked: Pozidriv four lines at 45 degrees to its arms, and Phillips
// nothing at all. (A JIS cross is the Phillips recess — see drill's
// Identification Marks.)
//
// A MARK BELONGS TO THE HEAD, NOT TO THE DRIVE. It is stamped after forming and
// it is not part of the recess, so a head carries one whichever drive is cut
// into it — and only the head knows the surface a mark has to follow, which is
// why these are overloads here rather than something the caller assembles. The
// mark is a plain 2D SECTION from drill: drill.tickSection or
// drill.dimpleSection. Passing the section as a value rather than a function
// keeps all of this clear of the closure machinery, which a head does not need.

// markHead: `head` with `markSection` engraved `markDepth` into its crown.
//
// Bounded to the upper half of the head, because a surface offset follows every
// surface the head has. A tick at radius 3 on a head of radius 6 sits over the
// bearing face as squarely as it sits over the crown, and a mark stamped into
// the face a screw pulls against is not a mark, it is a defect.
markHead(head, k, markSection, markDepth) {
  return max(head, -drill.engrave(head, markSection, markDepth, k / 2.0));
}

/// A cylindrical head carrying an identification mark — see markHead.
cylindricalHead(dk, k, markSection, markDepth) {
  return markHead(cylindricalHead(dk, k), k, markSection, markDepth);
}

/// A button head carrying an identification mark — see markHead.
buttonHead(dk, k, markSection, markDepth) {
  return markHead(buttonHead(dk, k), k, markSection, markDepth);
}

/// A pan head carrying an identification mark — the Pozidriv ticks, say. `markSection` is a 2D section from drill, engraved `markDepth` into
/// the crown and following it, so it reads at the rim of the dome as well as
/// at the middle.
///
///   fastener.panHead(12.0, 4.4, drill.dimpleSection(0.9, 4.4), 0.25)
panHead(dk, k, markSection, markDepth) {
  return markHead(panHead(dk, k), k, markSection, markDepth);
}

/// A pan head with its crown radius given and an identification mark — the
/// ISO 7045 head, catalog.panCrown, carrying Pozidriv ticks or any other mark.
panHead(dk, k, crownR, markSection, markDepth) {
  return markHead(panHead(dk, k, crownR), k, markSection, markDepth);
}

// =========================================================
// Shanks
// =========================================================
//
// A SHANK UNDER A HEAD RUNS UP INTO IT. A head bounded below by the seating
// plane and a shank bounded above by the same plane meet in a sheet inside the
// metal where both read zero, so their min touches zero there without changing
// sign: a surface of no thickness that a renderer, a mesher or a certify box can
// find. So every shank here takes a `zTop`, and every headed screw in this
// package passes half its head height — the overlap sits wholly inside the
// head, whose own surfaces are then the only ones there. The forms without
// `zTop` stop at the seating plane, which is right for a headless body: a set
// screw's top face IS the shank's top. The same goes for the joints inside a
// shank and a head — partialShank, shoulderShank and flangeHead each run the
// narrower piece into the wider one.

/// A PLAIN shank `dia` across hanging `shankLength` below the seating plane,
/// its tip chamfered, running up to `zTop` — inside the head it goes under.
/// The form to prove a fit against, whatever thread the screw is drawn with.
plainShank(dia, shankLength, zTop) {
  form = max(length(p.xy) - dia / 2.0, -shankLength - p.z, p.z - zTop,
             ((length(p.xy) - dia / 2.0)
              + (-p.z - shankLength) + 0.15 * dia) * 0.7071);
  form.box = [[-dia / 2.0, -dia / 2.0, -shankLength], [dia / 2.0, dia / 2.0, zTop]];
  return form;
}

/// A plain shank whose top is the seating plane: a headless body's.
plainShank(dia, shankLength) {
  return plainShank(dia, shankLength, 0.0);
}

/// A shank threaded its whole length: the ISO 68-1 external form at `pitchVal`,
/// crest at `dia`, running up to `zTop`. DISPLAY ONLY — prove against
/// plainShank, which is its envelope, or an interval proof subdivides to thread
/// scale to tell you what the plain cylinder already did.
threadedShank(dia, pitchVal, shankLength, zTop) {
  form = max(thread.male(dia, pitchVal), -shankLength - p.z, p.z - zTop,
             ((length(p.xy) - dia / 2.0)
              + (-p.z - shankLength) + 0.15 * dia) * 0.7071);
  form.box = [[-dia / 2.0, -dia / 2.0, -shankLength], [dia / 2.0, dia / 2.0, zTop]];
  return form;
}

/// A threaded shank whose top is the seating plane.
threadedShank(dia, pitchVal, shankLength) {
  return threadedShank(dia, pitchVal, shankLength, 0.0);
}

/// A shank threaded with a SINUSOID at the standard depth rather than the
/// standard V: the right envelope, the wrong flanks, and no corners in the
/// field. The cheap display form, running up to `zTop`.
sinShank(dia, pitchVal, shankLength, zTop) {
  form = max(thread.maleSin(dia, pitchVal), -shankLength - p.z, p.z - zTop,
             ((length(p.xy) - dia / 2.0)
              + (-p.z - shankLength) + 0.15 * dia) * 0.7071);
  form.box = [[-dia / 2.0, -dia / 2.0, -shankLength], [dia / 2.0, dia / 2.0, zTop]];
  return form;
}

/// A sinusoid shank whose top is the seating plane.
sinShank(dia, pitchVal, shankLength) {
  return sinShank(dia, pitchVal, shankLength, 0.0);
}

/// A PARTIALLY threaded shank: plain under the head, threaded for the last
/// `threadLength` of its `shankLength`. What a bolt is, as opposed to a screw
/// — the plain run is what takes shear in a fitted joint.
///
/// The thread runs up into the plain run, by a quarter of the diameter or half
/// the plain run, whichever is less, so the two do not meet on one plane.
partialShank(dia, pitchVal, shankLength, threadLength, zTop) {
  start = threadLength - shankLength;
  // No plain run at all when the thread is the longer, and then nothing to run into.
  into = max(0.0, min(0.25 * dia, -start / 2.0));
  plain = max(length(p.xy) - dia / 2.0, p.z - zTop, start - p.z);
  cut = max(thread.male(dia, pitchVal), -shankLength - p.z, p.z - (start + into),
            ((length(p.xy) - dia / 2.0)
             + (-p.z - shankLength) + 0.15 * dia) * 0.7071);
  form = min(plain, cut);
  // The plain run and the thread meet at start, wherever the lengths put it.
  form.box = [[-dia / 2.0, -dia / 2.0, min(-shankLength, start)],
              [dia / 2.0, dia / 2.0, max(zTop, start)]];
  return form;
}

/// A partially threaded shank whose top is the seating plane.
partialShank(dia, pitchVal, shankLength, threadLength) {
  return partialShank(dia, pitchVal, shankLength, threadLength, 0.0);
}

/// NO shank at all — the mirror of noHead(), and the head on its own.
///
/// It is what you want when the head and the shank have to be two OBJECTS
/// rather than one: a colour belongs to a named object, so a screw whose shank
/// is painted and whose head is not has to be built as two and min-ed, and this
/// is the half of that pair that carries the head.
noShank() {
  return nothing;
}

/// A SHOULDER shank: a ground shoulder `shoulderDia` across and
/// `shoulderLength` long, then a smaller thread. The shoulder is the bearing
/// surface — a shoulder screw is a dowel and a screw in one part, so the
/// shoulder is what is toleranced and the thread merely holds it there.
///
/// The stud runs up into the shoulder by a quarter of its diameter or half the
/// shoulder, whichever is less, so the two do not meet on one plane.
shoulderShank(shoulderDia, shoulderLength, threadDia, pitchVal, threadLength, zTop) {
  into = min(0.25 * threadDia, shoulderLength / 2.0);
  shoulder = max(length(p.xy) - shoulderDia / 2.0, -shoulderLength - p.z, p.z - zTop);
  stud = max(thread.male(threadDia, pitchVal),
             -(shoulderLength + threadLength) - p.z, p.z + shoulderLength - into,
             ((length(p.xy) - threadDia / 2.0)
              + (-p.z - shoulderLength - threadLength) + 0.15 * threadDia) * 0.7071);
  half = max(shoulderDia, threadDia) / 2.0;
  form = min(shoulder, stud);
  form.box = [[-half, -half, -(shoulderLength + threadLength)], [half, half, max(zTop, 0.0)]];
  return form;
}

/// A shoulder shank whose top is the seating plane.
shoulderShank(shoulderDia, shoulderLength, threadDia, pitchVal, threadLength) {
  return shoulderShank(shoulderDia, shoulderLength, threadDia, pitchVal, threadLength, 0.0);
}

// =========================================================
// Ends
// =========================================================
//
// A FOURTH AXIS, and it is not a set screw axis. A set screw is simply the
// fastener with nothing but an end — no head, and the point is the whole
// product — which is why the catalogues tabulate ends there and nowhere else.
// Every screw has one. A dog point on a shoulder screw, a cone on a pivot, a
// lead chamfer on a bolt so it starts in the hole: same shapes, same table.
//
// AN END IS TWO FIELDS, because ends both take material away and add it. A cup,
// a cone and an oval are ground into the stock; a dog point and a nylon tip
// stand proud of it. ended() takes one of each and nothing — the empty solid,
// the same value noHead() and noShank() return — is the one an end does not use.
//
// Every end takes the plane it sits on, so the same functions serve the bottom
// of a screw and the socket end of a set screw. They are VOIDS and SOLIDS, not
// closures: an end composes with min and max like everything else here.

/// `shank` finished with an end: `cut` taken out of it, `add` unioned onto it.
/// Pass nothing for whichever half the end does not use.
///
///   fastener.screw(fastener.noHead(),
///                  fastener.ended(fastener.threadedShank(6.0, 1.0, 10.0),
///                                 fastener.cupEnd(6.0, -10.0, 1.2, radians(118.0)),
///                                 nothing),
///                  drill.hexSocketAt(0.0, 3.0, 5.0))
ended(shank, cut, add) {
  return min(max(shank, -cut), add);
}

/// A FLAT end at `zEnd` with its edge broken by `leg` at 45 degrees. The plain
/// end, and the one every other end here is a modification of. A void.
flatEnd(dia, zEnd, leg) {
  return neg(((length(p.xy) - dia / 2.0) + (zEnd - p.z) + leg) * 0.7071);
}

/// The same break on the plane `zTop` with the material BELOW it — the chamfer
/// on the socket end of a set screw, which is not decoration: the screw is
/// driven down inside a threaded hole, and a sharp corner there catches on the
/// first thread and raises a burr that keeps it from seating.
flatTop(dia, zTop, leg) {
  return neg(((length(p.xy) - dia / 2.0) + (p.z - zTop) + leg) * 0.7071);
}

/// A CUP end: a cone ground into the end face to `depth`, leaving a thin
/// annular rim. The commonest set screw point and the best holder on a hard
/// shaft, because the rim bites rather than bearing flat. Pair it with a
/// flatEnd to break the rim's outside edge. A void.
cupEnd(dia, zEnd, depth, included) {
  return cone([0.0, 0.0, zEnd + depth], neg(vector.dirZ), included / 2.0);
}

/// A CONE end coming to a point at `zEnd`, `included` radians across. It digs
/// into the shaft and will not come out sideways, so it is the one for a
/// permanent setting — and for a spotted shaft, where the cone matches a
/// drilled detent. A void: the shank intersected with the cone.
coneEnd(dia, zEnd, included) {
  return neg(cone([0.0, 0.0, zEnd], vector.dirZ, included / 2.0));
}

/// An OVAL end: the tip radiused to `radius`, which is larger than the shank
/// radius, so it bears on a curved face without the bite of a cone. The point
/// for a shaft that has to be adjusted and re-set without chewing it up. A void.
///
/// IT HAS TO BE BOUNDED ABOVE, and a cone does not. A cone opening upward from
/// the tip is wider than the shank everywhere above the point, so intersecting
/// with it takes nothing there — but a SPHERE closes again, and an unbounded
/// version of this end quietly deletes the whole screw above the ball's
/// equator. So the void stops at zJoin, where the sphere crosses the shank's
/// own diameter, and above that the shank is left alone.
ovalEnd(dia, zEnd, radius) {
  half = dia / 2.0;
  zJoin = zEnd + radius - sqrt(radius * radius - half * half);
  return max(neg(length([p.x, p.y, p.z - (zEnd + radius)]) - radius),
             p.z - zJoin);
}

/// A BALL end: the oval end at exactly the shank radius, so the tip is a true
/// hemisphere. Bears on a single point, which is what a ball-end set screw in a
/// V-groove or against a flat is for.
ballEnd(dia, zEnd) {
  return ovalEnd(dia, zEnd, dia / 2.0);
}

/// A DOG POINT — an EXTENDED point: an unthreaded cylinder `pointDia` across
/// standing `pointLength` below `zEnd`. It drops into a hole or a slot in the
/// shaft, so the screw locates as well as clamps, and it does not mark the
/// bearing surface the way a cone does. A SOLID, so it goes in ended()'s `add`.
///
/// A half dog point is the same thing shorter. The extended point in a softer
/// material — brass or nylon, so it clamps without marking — is the same solid
/// too; make it a separate named object if it wants its own colour.
dogPoint(pointDia, zEnd, pointLength) {
  // Up into the shank a quarter of its own diameter, not stopped on the end
  // face: see Shanks on two solids that meet on one plane.
  form = max(length(p.xy) - pointDia / 2.0,
             (zEnd - pointLength) - p.z, p.z - (zEnd + 0.25 * pointDia),
             ((length(p.xy) - pointDia / 2.0)
              + ((zEnd - pointLength) - p.z) + 0.15 * pointDia) * 0.7071);
  form.box = [[-pointDia / 2.0, -pointDia / 2.0, zEnd - pointLength],
              [pointDia / 2.0, pointDia / 2.0, zEnd + 0.25 * pointDia]];
  return form;
}

/// A SOFT TIP: a nylon or brass pad `tipLength` long on the end of the screw,
/// clamping without marking the shaft and locking against vibration.
///
/// `dia` IS THE THREAD'S MINOR DIAMETER, not its nominal. The pad sits in a
/// pocket bored into the end of the screw, and that pocket cannot be wider than
/// the root of the thread without cutting through it — a pad at nominal would
/// stand out past the crests and be sheared off going into the hole. Pass
/// thread.minorDia(major, pitch).
///
/// A SOLID, and one you should keep as its own named object rather than folding
/// into the screw, because the whole point of it is that it is a different
/// material — and a colour belongs to a named object.
softTip(dia, zEnd, tipLength) {
  // Up into the body a quarter of its own diameter — the pad is set into the
  // screw, and two solids stopped on one plane meet in a sheet of no thickness.
  form = max(length(p.xy) - dia / 2.0,
             (zEnd - tipLength) - p.z, p.z - (zEnd + 0.25 * dia),
             ((length(p.xy) - dia / 2.0)
              + ((zEnd - tipLength) - p.z) + 0.2 * dia) * 0.7071);
  form.box = [[-dia / 2.0, -dia / 2.0, zEnd - tipLength], [dia / 2.0, dia / 2.0, zEnd + 0.25 * dia]];
  return form;
}

// drillFlutes: two flutes ground into a point, 180 degrees apart and each
// reaching the axis, so the point has two cutting lips and a chisel edge
// between them — which is what makes it cut rather than just push.
//
// Offset by half their width rather than centred, so the pair is a rotation of
// one another rather than a single slot straight through: a slot through the
// centre would leave two lips facing the same way and cut on one side only.
drillFlutes(width, zEnd, fluteLength) {
  one = max(abs(p.y - width / 2.0) - width / 2.0, neg(p.x),
            p.z - (zEnd + fluteLength));
  return min(one, remap(one, [neg(p.x), neg(p.y), p.z]));
}

/// A SELF-DRILLING point: an UNTHREADED drill standing `pointLength` below
/// `zEnd`, at `dia`, ending in a conical point of `included` angle, with two
/// flutes running from the tip up to where the thread starts.
///
/// A SOLID — it stands proud of the screw — so it goes in ended()'s `add`.
///
/// `dia` IS THE THREAD'S MINOR DIAMETER. That is the whole mechanism: the point
/// drills a hole the size of the thread's ROOT, and the thread then cuts its own
/// crests into the wall of that hole. A drill at nominal would bore away the
/// material the thread needs to bite and the screw would spin in its own hole.
/// Pass thread.minorDia(major, pitch).
///
/// THE FLUTES STOP AT THE THREAD. They have to: chips have to clear the cutting
/// lips and get out of the hole, and past the first thread there is nothing left
/// to cut, so a flute run up into the thread would only be a notch out of it.
///
/// The flutes are straight here where a real point's are helical. That is
/// enough to draw the screw and to see which way it cuts; it is not a tool
/// geometry, and nothing here should be used to grind one.
selfDrillPoint(dia, zEnd, pointLength, included, fluteWidth) {
  tip = zEnd - pointLength;
  // Up into the shank a quarter of the diameter; see dogPoint.
  body = max(length(p.xy) - dia / 2.0,
             cone([0.0, 0.0, tip], vector.dirZ, included / 2.0),
             p.z - (zEnd + 0.25 * dia));
  form = max(body, -drillFlutes(fluteWidth, tip, pointLength));
  form.box = [[-dia / 2.0, -dia / 2.0, tip], [dia / 2.0, dia / 2.0, zEnd + 0.25 * dia]];
  return form;
}

// =========================================================
// Composition
// =========================================================

/// A screw: the `head` and `shank` solids unioned, with the `drive` recess
/// taken out. Each is an ordinary field, so any head goes with any shank and
/// any drive — drill.hexSocketAt, drill.crossRecessAt, drill.squareSocketAt
/// and drill.slotDriveAt all cut one.
///
///   fastener.screw(fastener.panHead(9.5, 3.3),
///                  fastener.threadedShank(5.0, 0.8, 16.0),
///                  drill.crossRecessAt(3.3, 4.4, 1.2, 2.2, radians(7.0)))
screw(head, shank, drive) {
  return max(min(head, shank), -drive);
}

/// A screw with no drive recess — an external drive turns on the head itself,
/// so a hex head needs nothing cut into it.
screw(head, shank) {
  return min(head, shank);
}

// =========================================================
// Multi-Material Set Screws
// =========================================================
//
// TWO OBJECTS, NOT ONE, and that is the whole point of these. A nylon-tipped or
// brass-tipped set screw is two materials, and a material is a fact about a
// named object — a colour belongs to a name, and so does anything else you
// would later hang off one. Folding the tip into the screw would throw away the
// only thing that makes it a multi-material part.
//
// So these MULTIRETURN: the steel body first, the tip second.
//
//   body, tip = fastener.setScrewSoftTip(6.0, 10.0, 3.0, 5.5, 2.0);
//   body.color = steel;
//   tip.color = nylon;
//   return min(body, tip);
//
// The two are built on one datum and one set of dimensions, so they cannot
// drift apart — which is what went wrong when the painted thread in the
// Fastener Cosmetics example was a colour on a whole screw instead of a
// colour on a shank.

// setScrewBody: the common half of every set screw here — threaded its whole
// length, socket-side chamfer at z = 0, hex socket down the middle. Its end is
// left open for the caller to finish.
setScrewBody(nominalDia, screwLength, socketAf, socketDepth, endCut, endAdd) {
  pitchVal = thread.metricPitch(nominalDia);
  brk = 0.08 * nominalDia;
  return screw(noHead(),
               ended(threadedShank(nominalDia, pitchVal, screwLength),
                     min(flatTop(nominalDia, 0.0, brk), endCut),
                     endAdd),
               drill.hexSocketAt(0.0, socketAf, socketDepth));
}

/// A SET SCREW WITH A SOFT TIP, as two objects: the steel body, then the nylon
/// or brass pad on the end of it. Multireturn — see the note above.
///
/// The pad is sized on the thread's MINOR diameter, because it sits in a pocket
/// bored into the end and that pocket cannot be wider than the thread root
/// without cutting through the crests.
setScrewSoftTip(nominalDia, screwLength, socketAf, socketDepth, tipLength) {
  pitchVal = thread.metricPitch(nominalDia);
  zEnd = 0.0 - screwLength;
  minor = thread.minorDia(nominalDia, pitchVal);
  body = setScrewBody(nominalDia, screwLength, socketAf, socketDepth,
                      flatEnd(nominalDia, zEnd, 0.08 * nominalDia), nothing);
  body.box = [[-nominalDia / 2.0, -nominalDia / 2.0, zEnd],
              [nominalDia / 2.0, nominalDia / 2.0, 0.0]];
  return body, softTip(minor, zEnd, tipLength);
}

/// A SET SCREW WITH AN EXTENDED POINT in a second material — the brass or nylon
/// dog point that locates and clamps without marking the shaft. Two objects:
/// the steel body, then the point.
setScrewSoftPoint(nominalDia, screwLength, socketAf, socketDepth,
                  pointDia, pointLength) {
  zEnd = 0.0 - screwLength;
  body = setScrewBody(nominalDia, screwLength, socketAf, socketDepth,
                      flatEnd(nominalDia, zEnd, 0.08 * nominalDia), nothing);
  body.box = [[-nominalDia / 2.0, -nominalDia / 2.0, zEnd],
              [nominalDia / 2.0, nominalDia / 2.0, 0.0]];
  return body, dogPoint(pointDia, zEnd, pointLength);
}

/// A SELF-DRILLING SCREW as two objects: the threaded body, then the drill
/// point. Two because the point is usually a harder or case-hardened material
/// than the body — it has to cut the hole the body then threads.
setScrewDrillPoint(nominalDia, screwLength, socketAf, socketDepth,
                   pointLength, included, fluteWidth) {
  pitchVal = thread.metricPitch(nominalDia);
  zEnd = 0.0 - screwLength;
  minor = thread.minorDia(nominalDia, pitchVal);
  body = setScrewBody(nominalDia, screwLength, socketAf, socketDepth,
                      nothing, nothing);
  body.box = [[-nominalDia / 2.0, -nominalDia / 2.0, zEnd],
              [nominalDia / 2.0, nominalDia / 2.0, 0.0]];
  return body, selfDrillPoint(minor, zEnd, pointLength, included, fluteWidth);
}

// =========================================================
// Standard Screws
// =========================================================

/// ISO 4762 socket head cap screw at catalog nominal: cylindrical head
/// (diameter and hex socket from the size table, height = nominal) above
/// z = 0, plain shank of the given length below, 45-degree tip chamfer.
/// fastener.shcs(3.0, 8.0) is an M3 x 8. Seating plane at z = 0; place
/// with remap(). Prove clearances against this form; display
/// shcsThreaded.
shcs(nominalDia, shankLength) {
  headH = nominalDia;
  half = max(catalog.shcsHeadDia(nominalDia), nominalDia) / 2.0;
  form = screw(cylindricalHead(catalog.shcsHeadDia(nominalDia), headH),
               plainShank(nominalDia, shankLength, headH / 2.0),
               drill.hexSocketAt(headH, catalog.shcsSocket(nominalDia),
                                 catalog.shcsSocketDepth(nominalDia)));
  form.box = [[-half, -half, -shankLength], [half, half, headH]];
  form.simple = shcsStand(nominalDia, shankLength);
  return form;
}

/// ISO 4762 socket head cap screw with a display-only helical thread on
/// the shank: the real ISO 68-1 external form at the coarse pitch, crest at
/// nominal. Same dimensions and datum as shcs — use it in display returns;
/// prove against shcs.
shcsThreaded(nominalDia, shankLength) {
  headH = nominalDia;
  half = max(catalog.shcsHeadDia(nominalDia), nominalDia) / 2.0;
  form = screw(cylindricalHead(catalog.shcsHeadDia(nominalDia), headH),
               threadedShank(nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia), shankLength, headH / 2.0),
               drill.hexSocketAt(headH, catalog.shcsSocket(nominalDia),
                                 catalog.shcsSocketDepth(nominalDia)));
  form.box = [[-half, -half, -shankLength], [half, half, headH]];
  form.color = plainSteel();
  stand = shcsStand(nominalDia, shankLength);
  stand.color = malePaint(nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia), 0.0, 0.0 - shankLength, threadIso);
  form.simple = stand;
  return form;
}

/// ISO 4762 socket head cap screw whose shank thread is a SINUSOID rather
/// than the standard V — the cheap display form, with no corners in its
/// field. Same dimensions and datum as shcs; prove against shcs.
shcsThreadedSin(nominalDia, shankLength) {
  headH = nominalDia;
  half = max(catalog.shcsHeadDia(nominalDia), nominalDia) / 2.0;
  form = screw(cylindricalHead(catalog.shcsHeadDia(nominalDia), headH),
               sinShank(nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia), shankLength, headH / 2.0),
               drill.hexSocketAt(headH, catalog.shcsSocket(nominalDia),
                                 catalog.shcsSocketDepth(nominalDia)));
  form.box = [[-half, -half, -shankLength], [half, half, headH]];
  form.color = plainSteel();
  stand = shcsStand(nominalDia, shankLength);
  stand.color = malePaint(nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia), 0.0, 0.0 - shankLength, threadSin);
  form.simple = stand;
  return form;
}

/// ISO 10642 countersunk (flat head) screw: 90-degree conical head flush
/// with the surface at z = 0, ISO 10642's own hex socket (a size smaller than
/// shcs's, cut to its minimum key engagement), shank to the given overall
/// length below. Mates with fastener.countersunkHole. Prove against this
/// form; display countersunkThreaded.
countersunk(nominalDia, overallLength) {
  dk = catalog.cskHeadDia(nominalDia);
  half = max(dk, nominalDia) / 2.0;
  form = screw(countersunkHead(catalog.cskHeadDia(nominalDia), nominalDia),
               plainShank(nominalDia, overallLength),
               drill.hexSocketAt(0.0, catalog.cskSocket(nominalDia),
                                 catalog.cskSocketDepth(nominalDia)));
  form.box = [[-half, -half, -max(overallLength, (dk - nominalDia) / 2.0)],
              [half, half, 0.0]];
  form.simple = countersunkStand(nominalDia, overallLength);
  return form;
}

/// ISO 10642 countersunk screw with a display-only helical thread on the
/// shank. Same dimensions and datum as countersunk.
countersunkThreaded(nominalDia, overallLength) {
  dk = catalog.cskHeadDia(nominalDia);
  half = max(dk, nominalDia) / 2.0;
  form = screw(countersunkHead(catalog.cskHeadDia(nominalDia), nominalDia),
               threadedShank(nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia), overallLength),
               drill.hexSocketAt(0.0, catalog.cskSocket(nominalDia),
                                 catalog.cskSocketDepth(nominalDia)));
  form.box = [[-half, -half, -max(overallLength, (dk - nominalDia) / 2.0)],
              [half, half, 0.0]];
  form.color = plainSteel();
  stand = countersunkStand(nominalDia, overallLength);
  stand.color = malePaint(nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia), 0.0 - (dk - nominalDia) / 2.0,
                          0.0 - overallLength, threadIso);
  form.simple = stand;
  return form;
}

// =========================================================
// More Standard Screws
// =========================================================
//
// Each of these is what shcs is: catalog lookups and one screw() call, smooth
// shank, seating plane at z = 0. Prove against them as they are; display the
// thread with thread.maleRelief on the part's material.

// crownAt: the height of a pan head's domed crown at radius r — which is where
// ISO 4757 measures a recess from. Its reference plane passes through the
// points where the recess meets the top of the head, and on a domed head those
// sit below the crown's apex, where the dome crosses the recess diameter.
crownAt(k, crownR, r) {
  return k - crownR + sqrt(max(crownR * crownR - r * r, 0.0));
}

// ONE RECESS NUMBER IS ONE FORM. ISO 4757 draws a type H recess once per
// number — four ribs left standing in a 26 degree 30 cone over a pointed
// bottom — and the product standard's m sinks it: the cone crosses the
// reference plane at m, so a larger m puts the same form deeper. All three PH2
// pan heads are one shape at three depths, which is what lets one driver fit
// them all. ISO 7045's gauge penetration is not the depth; it is the check.
// The gauge, seated, has to reach between its minimum and its maximum.

/// The z of the REFERENCE PLANE of an ISO 7045 type H recess in a pan head of
/// this size: where the recess meets the top of the head, below the crown's
/// apex, and what ISO 4757 measures penetration from.
panPhillipsReference(nominalDia) {
  k = catalog.panHeadHeight(nominalDia);
  crownR = catalog.panCrown(nominalDia);
  return crownAt(k, crownR, catalog.panPhillipsDia(nominalDia) / 2.0);
}

/// The z of the deepest point ISO 7045 lets a type H gauge reach in a pan head
/// of this size: the maximum penetration below the reference plane. The
/// recess itself runs on below it to the point of its 28 degree bottom.
panPhillipsFloor(nominalDia) {
  k = catalog.panHeadHeight(nominalDia);
  crownR = catalog.panCrown(nominalDia);
  return crownAt(k, crownR, catalog.panPhillipsDia(nominalDia) / 2.0)
         - catalog.panPhillipsDepth(nominalDia);
}

/// The z to SEAT a Phillips driver's point at in a pan head of this size:
/// ISO 7045's minimum gauge penetration below the reference plane. The gauge
/// is proven clear of the recess there and touches before the maximum, so a
/// driver.phillipsTip put here is in the recess, a few hundredths off its ribs.
panPhillipsSeat(nominalDia) {
  return panPhillipsReference(nominalDia) - catalog.panPhillipsDepthMin(nominalDia);
}

/// The z of the floor of the ISO 14583 hexalobular recess in a pan head of
/// this size — where a Torx driver's end seats.
panTorxFloor(nominalDia) {
  k = catalog.panHeadHeight(nominalDia);
  crownR = catalog.panCrown(nominalDia);
  t = catalog.panTorx(nominalDia);
  return crownAt(k, crownR, catalog.torxMajor(t) / 2.0) - catalog.panTorxDepth(nominalDia);
}

/// The z of the floor of an ISO 7045 type Z recess in a pan head of this size.
panPozidrivFloor(nominalDia) {
  k = catalog.panHeadHeight(nominalDia);
  crownR = catalog.panCrown(nominalDia);
  return crownAt(k, crownR, catalog.panPozidrivDia(nominalDia) / 2.0)
         - catalog.panPozidrivDepth(nominalDia);
}

/// The ISO 7045 type H (PHILLIPS) recess of a pan head of this size, as the
/// void panPhillips cuts: seated on the plane ISO 4757 measures it from, m
/// across there, in the ISO 4757 form of its recess number — see
/// drill.phillipsAt. For a head that carries it with something else — a mark,
/// a different shank.
panPhillipsDrive(nominalDia) {
  no = catalog.panRecess(nominalDia);
  return drill.phillipsAt(panPhillipsReference(nominalDia),
                          catalog.panPhillipsDia(nominalDia),
                          catalog.phillipsCore(no), catalog.phillipsFacet(no),
                          catalog.phillipsPoint(no), catalog.phillipsRidge(no),
                          catalog.phillipsFlank(no), catalog.phillipsRoot(no));
}

/// The ISO 4757 type H GAUGE for a pan head of this size, seated `pen` below
/// its reference plane and standing 10 above it: the point of the screwdriver
/// the recess is checked with, as a solid.
panPhillipsGauge(nominalDia, pen) {
  no = catalog.panRecess(nominalDia);
  zRef = panPhillipsReference(nominalDia);
  return drill.phillipsGauge(zRef - pen, zRef + 10.0,
                             catalog.phillipsGaugeCore(no), catalog.phillipsGaugeFacet(no),
                             catalog.phillipsGaugePoint(no), catalog.phillipsGaugeFlat(no),
                             catalog.phillipsGaugeRidge(no), catalog.phillipsGaugeFlank(no),
                             catalog.phillipsGaugeRoot(no));
}

/// The z of the REFERENCE PLANE of an ISO 7045 type Z recess in a pan head of
/// this size, where penetration is measured from.
panPozidrivReference(nominalDia) {
  k = catalog.panHeadHeight(nominalDia);
  crownR = catalog.panCrown(nominalDia);
  return crownAt(k, crownR, catalog.panPozidrivDia(nominalDia) / 2.0);
}

/// The z to SEAT a Pozidriv driver's point at in a pan head of this size:
/// ISO 7045's minimum type Z penetration below the reference plane, where the
/// gauge is proven clear and short of touching.
panPozidrivSeat(nominalDia) {
  return panPozidrivReference(nominalDia) - catalog.panPozidrivDepthMin(nominalDia);
}

/// The ISO 7045 type Z (POZIDRIV) recess of a pan head of this size, as the
/// void panPozidriv cuts: seated on the reference plane, m across there, in the
/// ISO 4757 form of its recess number — see drill.pozidrivAt.
panPozidrivDrive(nominalDia) {
  no = catalog.panRecess(nominalDia);
  return drill.pozidrivAt(panPozidrivReference(nominalDia),
                          catalog.panPozidrivDia(nominalDia),
                          catalog.pozidrivCore(no), catalog.pozidrivWing(no),
                          catalog.pozidrivKnee(no), catalog.pozidrivLean(no),
                          catalog.pozidrivValley(no), catalog.pozidrivMarkDepth(no));
}

/// The ISO 4757 type Z GAUGE for a pan head of this size, its point `pen`
/// below the reference plane and standing 10 above it.
panPozidrivGauge(nominalDia, pen) {
  no = catalog.panRecess(nominalDia);
  point = catalog.pozidrivGaugePoint(no);
  zRef = panPozidrivReference(nominalDia);
  return drill.pozidrivGauge(zRef - pen + point, zRef + 10.0,
                             catalog.pozidrivGaugeCore(no), catalog.pozidrivGaugeWing(no),
                             catalog.pozidrivGaugeKnee(no), point,
                             catalog.pozidrivLean(no), catalog.pozidrivValley(no));
}

/// ISO 7045 cross recessed PAN HEAD screw, type H — PHILLIPS — at catalog
/// nominal, M1.6 to M10: fastener.panPhillips(4.0, 10.0) is an M4 x 10 with a
/// PH2. Head, recess number, recess diameter and depth all from the standard;
/// the recess is panPhillipsDrive.
panPhillips(nominalDia, shankLength) {
  half = max(catalog.panHeadDia(nominalDia), nominalDia) / 2.0;
  form = screw(panHead(catalog.panHeadDia(nominalDia), catalog.panHeadHeight(nominalDia),
                       catalog.panCrown(nominalDia)),
               plainShank(nominalDia, shankLength, catalog.panHeadHeight(nominalDia) / 2.0),
               panPhillipsDrive(nominalDia));
  form.box = [[-half, -half, -shankLength],
              [half, half, catalog.panHeadHeight(nominalDia)]];
  form.simple = panStand(nominalDia, shankLength);
  return form;
}

/// ISO 7045 cross recessed pan head screw, type Z — POZIDRIV. The same head
/// as panPhillips; the recess is panPozidrivDrive.
panPozidriv(nominalDia, shankLength) {
  half = max(catalog.panHeadDia(nominalDia), nominalDia) / 2.0;
  form = screw(panHead(catalog.panHeadDia(nominalDia), catalog.panHeadHeight(nominalDia),
                       catalog.panCrown(nominalDia)),
               plainShank(nominalDia, shankLength, catalog.panHeadHeight(nominalDia) / 2.0),
               panPozidrivDrive(nominalDia));
  form.box = [[-half, -half, -shankLength],
              [half, half, catalog.panHeadHeight(nominalDia)]];
  form.simple = panStand(nominalDia, shankLength);
  return form;
}

/// ISO 14583 hexalobular socket PAN HEAD screw, M2 to M10: ISO 7045's head
/// with the TORX socket ISO 14583 pairs with each size.
panTorx(nominalDia, shankLength) {
  k = catalog.panHeadHeight(nominalDia);
  crownR = catalog.panCrown(nominalDia);
  t = catalog.panTorx(nominalDia);
  major = catalog.torxMajor(t);
  half = max(catalog.panHeadDia(nominalDia), nominalDia) / 2.0;
  form = screw(panHead(catalog.panHeadDia(nominalDia), k, crownR),
               plainShank(nominalDia, shankLength, k / 2.0),
               drill.torxAt(crownAt(k, crownR, major / 2.0), major, catalog.torxMinor(t),
                            catalog.panTorxDepth(nominalDia)));
  form.box = [[-half, -half, -shankLength], [half, half, k]];
  form.simple = panStand(nominalDia, shankLength);
  return form;
}

/// ISO 7380-1 hexagon socket BUTTON HEAD screw, M3 to M16. The crown follows
/// from the head's span and height, and lands inside the radius range the
/// standard gives for it.
button(nominalDia, shankLength) {
  k = catalog.buttonHeadHeight(nominalDia);
  // Every ISO 7380 head is flatter than a hemisphere, so it is widest at the
  // seating plane, the head diameter across.
  half = max(catalog.buttonHeadDia(nominalDia), nominalDia) / 2.0;
  form = screw(buttonHead(catalog.buttonHeadDia(nominalDia), k),
               plainShank(nominalDia, shankLength, k / 2.0),
               drill.hexSocketAt(k, catalog.buttonSocket(nominalDia),
                                 catalog.buttonSocketDepth(nominalDia)));
  form.box = [[-half, -half, -shankLength], [half, half, k]];
  form.simple = min(buttonHead(catalog.buttonHeadDia(nominalDia), k),
                    standShank(nominalDia, shankLength, k / 2.0));
  return form;
}

/// ISO 14579 hexalobular socket head cap screw: ISO 4762's head with the TORX
/// socket the standard pairs with each size, M2 to M12.
shcsTorx(nominalDia, shankLength) {
  headH = nominalDia;
  t = catalog.shcsTorx(nominalDia);
  half = max(catalog.shcsHeadDia(nominalDia), nominalDia) / 2.0;
  form = screw(cylindricalHead(catalog.shcsHeadDia(nominalDia), headH),
               plainShank(nominalDia, shankLength, headH / 2.0),
               drill.torxAt(headH, catalog.torxMajor(t), catalog.torxMinor(t),
                            catalog.shcsTorxDepth(nominalDia)));
  form.box = [[-half, -half, -shankLength], [half, half, headH]];
  form.simple = shcsStand(nominalDia, shankLength);
  return form;
}

/// ISO 4017 HEXAGON HEAD SCREW, threaded to the head: M1.6 to M12, M16, M20
/// and M24. An external drive, so nothing is cut into the head.
hexScrew(nominalDia, shankLength) {
  af = catalog.hexHeadAcrossFlats(nominalDia);
  halfX = max(af, nominalDia) / 2.0;
  halfY = max(af * 0.57735027, nominalDia / 2.0);
  form = screw(hexHead(catalog.hexHeadAcrossFlats(nominalDia),
                       catalog.hexHeadHeight(nominalDia)),
               plainShank(nominalDia, shankLength, catalog.hexHeadHeight(nominalDia) / 2.0));
  form.box = [[-halfX, -halfY, -shankLength],
              [halfX, halfY, catalog.hexHeadHeight(nominalDia)]];
  form.simple = hexStand(nominalDia, shankLength);
  return form;
}

/// ISO 4014 HEXAGON HEAD BOLT: the hex screw's head on a shank threaded only
/// its last hexBoltThread(nominalDia, shankLength).
///
/// THE SMOOTH FORM IS THE SCREW'S. A bolt and a screw are the same solid at the
/// major diameter; what differs is how much of the shank is threaded, and that
/// is the part displayed rather than proved — pass the thread length to
/// thread.maleRelief as its bound, or to partialShank.
hexBolt(nominalDia, shankLength) {
  return hexScrew(nominalDia, shankLength);
}

/// The threaded length b of an ISO 4014 bolt: 2d + 6 up to 125 long, 2d + 12
/// over 125 to 200, and 2d + 25 over 200, or the whole shank if that is
/// shorter. The rule reproduces every b ISO 4014 tabulates but one: an M1.6
/// is 9, not 9.2. The standard leaves b blank where no bolt of that length is
/// made — b1 from M36, b2 at M56 and M64 — and this still answers there.
///
/// REFERENCES: ISO 4014:2022 Tables 1-4 (M1.6 to M27) and :2011 Table 1 (M12
/// to M64), the boundaries from their footnotes,
/// https://cdn.standards.iteh.ai/samples/72579/81f2b1e966b440d7a25f6734e508ea48/ISO-4014-2022.pdf
/// https://cdn.standards.iteh.ai/samples/56447/63b5e00f45d043e6a27a45ae955b88f9/ISO-4014-2011.pdf
hexBoltThread(nominalDia, shankLength) {
  b = 2.0 * nominalDia + 6.0
      + 6.0 * clamp(sign(shankLength - 125.0), 0.0, 1.0)
      + 13.0 * clamp(sign(shankLength - 200.0), 0.0, 1.0)
      - 0.2 * catalog.onRow(nominalDia, 1.6, 0.0001);
  return min(b, shankLength);
}

// =========================================================
// Inch Square Drive Screws
// =========================================================
//
// ASME B18.6.3's square recessed machine screws — the ROBERTSON — which the
// square recess exists for: it has no metric screw to go in. Sized in INCHES
// by nominal diameter, 0.164 for a #8, the key thread.uncPitch and the catalog
// share; built, like everything else here, in millimetres, so the shank
// length is in millimetres too — a #8 x 1/2" is (0.164, 12.7). The recess is
// the one drill.robertsonAt the rest of the library draws, the pyramid bottom
// running out from the floor, cut to the depth the product table gives.

/// A PAN head the way ASME B18.6.3 dimensions it: a cylinder `dk` across and
/// `k` tall whose top edge is rounded to `edge`, flat on top — the standard
/// gives the edge and not the crown. Exact: the offset, by `edge`, of the
/// corner the flat top and the side make.
roundedHead(dk, k, edge) {
  q = [length(p.xy) - (dk / 2.0 - edge), p.z - (k - edge)];
  return max(length([max(q.x, 0.0), max(q.y, 0.0)]) + min(max(q.x, q.y), 0.0) - edge,
             -p.z);
}

/// ASME B18.6.3 type III square recessed PAN HEAD machine screw, #4 to 1/4",
/// nominal diameter in INCHES and shank length in millimetres:
/// fastener.panRobertson(0.164, 12.7) is a #8 x 1/2" with a #2 recess. Seating
/// plane at z = 0, shank down, like every screw here.
panRobertson(nominalInch, shankLength) {
  k = catalog.inchPanHeadHeight(nominalInch);
  return screw(roundedHead(catalog.inchPanHeadDia(nominalInch), k,
                           catalog.inchPanEdge(nominalInch)),
               plainShank(nominalInch * 25.4, shankLength, k / 2.0),
               drill.robertsonAt(k, catalog.robertsonAcross(catalog.inchPanRecess(nominalInch)),
                                 catalog.inchPanRecessDepth(nominalInch)));
}

/// ASME B18.6.3 type III square recessed 82-degree FLAT HEAD machine screw, #4
/// to 1/4", nominal diameter in INCHES and overall length in millimetres — a
/// flat head's length is overall, from the top face. The head sits BELOW z = 0
/// and seats flush, as countersunk() does.
flatRobertson(nominalInch, overallLength) {
  return screw(countersunkHead(catalog.inchFlatHeadDia(nominalInch), nominalInch * 25.4,
                               radians(82.0)),
               plainShank(nominalInch * 25.4, overallLength),
               drill.robertsonAt(0.0, catalog.robertsonAcross(catalog.inchFlatRecess(nominalInch)),
                                 catalog.inchFlatRecessDepth(nominalInch)));
}

// =========================================================
// Placement
// =========================================================

// The library datum puts a screw's head above z = 0 with its shank hanging
// DOWN, which is right for a screw dropped into a part from above. A screw
// driven UP into the part it fastens — the through-bolted joint, where the
// head sits in a counterbore underneath — needs the datum turned over, and a
// remap that translates without turning it silently puts the whole screw on
// the wrong side of the joint.

// TURNED OVER, NOT MIRRORED. Negating z alone reflects the screw, and a
// reflected right-hand thread is a left-hand one; negating y with it is a
// half turn about x, which every screw survives unchanged. These helpers take the seat and the direction
// the shank runs, so there is no transform left to get backwards.

/// The remap coordinate that seats a +Z-datum fastener at `seatPoint` with
/// its shank running along `shankDir`, any direction: vector.dirZ drives the
/// screw upward, its negation keeps the library's own downward datum, and a
/// face normal's negation drives it square into that face. Pass it to remap:
/// `remap(fastener.shcs(3.0, 18.0), fastener.seatedAt([8, 26.75, -4.5], vector.dirZ))`.
///
/// It is map.axisAt with the axis reversed — the datum's +Z is the way the
/// head faces, opposite the shank — so a screw seated here lands in a hole
/// placed by drill.axisAt at the same point with the face's normal. Straight
/// up it is a half turn about x, never a mirror. As with axisAt, the turn
/// ABOUT the shank is not continuous across the equator; a round screw does
/// not show it, a hex head does.
seatedAt(seatPoint, shankDir) {
  return map.axisAt(seatPoint, [neg(shankDir.x), neg(shankDir.y), neg(shankDir.z)]);
}

/// An ISO 4762 socket head cap screw already placed: its head seated at
/// `seatPoint` with the shank running along `shankDir`. Identical geometry
/// to shcs(), so prove against it the same way; shcsSeatedThreaded is the
/// display twin. Sizing rule for a through-bolted joint: the shank must
/// cross every part it passes through AND engage at least three pitches of
/// thread in the part it holds — a catalog screw that stops short of that
/// part fastens nothing.
shcsSeated(nominalDia, shankLength, seatPoint, shankDir) {
  // shcs's box moved to the seat, and turned over with the screw when its
  // shank runs up: the flip is seatedAt's, and the datum's z from -shankLength
  // to the head's top lands on either side of the seat.
  flip = 1.0 - 2.0 * clamp(sign(shankDir.z) + 1.0, 0.0, 1.0);
  half = max(catalog.shcsHeadDia(nominalDia), nominalDia) / 2.0;
  zA = seatPoint.z - flip * shankLength;
  zB = seatPoint.z + flip * nominalDia;
  form = remap(shcs(nominalDia, shankLength), seatedAt(seatPoint, shankDir));
  form.box = [[seatPoint.x - half, seatPoint.y - half, min(zA, zB)],
              [seatPoint.x + half, seatPoint.y + half, max(zA, zB)]];
  return form;
}

/// shcsSeated with the display-only helical thread — same dimensions and
/// placement as shcsSeated; use it in display returns, prove against
/// shcsSeated.
shcsSeatedThreaded(nominalDia, shankLength, seatPoint, shankDir) {
  // shcsSeated's box.
  flip = 1.0 - 2.0 * clamp(sign(shankDir.z) + 1.0, 0.0, 1.0);
  half = max(catalog.shcsHeadDia(nominalDia), nominalDia) / 2.0;
  zA = seatPoint.z - flip * shankLength;
  zB = seatPoint.z + flip * nominalDia;
  form = remap(shcsThreaded(nominalDia, shankLength), seatedAt(seatPoint, shankDir));
  form.box = [[seatPoint.x - half, seatPoint.y - half, min(zA, zB)],
              [seatPoint.x + half, seatPoint.y + half, max(zA, zB)]];
  return form;
}

/// An ISO 10642 countersunk screw already placed: its head flush with the
/// surface at `seatPoint`, shank running along `shankDir`. Mates with
/// fastener.countersunkHole placed with the same seatedAt.
countersunkSeated(nominalDia, overallLength, seatPoint, shankDir) {
  // countersunk's box, placed as shcsSeated places shcs's.
  flip = 1.0 - 2.0 * clamp(sign(shankDir.z) + 1.0, 0.0, 1.0);
  dk = catalog.cskHeadDia(nominalDia);
  half = max(dk, nominalDia) / 2.0;
  zA = seatPoint.z - flip * max(overallLength, (dk - nominalDia) / 2.0);
  form = remap(countersunk(nominalDia, overallLength), seatedAt(seatPoint, shankDir));
  form.box = [[seatPoint.x - half, seatPoint.y - half, min(zA, seatPoint.z)],
              [seatPoint.x + half, seatPoint.y + half, max(zA, seatPoint.z)]];
  return form;
}

// =========================================================
// Nuts, Washers and Pins
// =========================================================

/// ISO 4032 hex nut at catalog nominal: hex prism (width across flats and
/// height from the size table) resting on z = 0, corner chamfers on both
/// faces, and the bore at the coarse thread's basic MINOR diameter — the
/// nut's maximum material condition, which the screw through it interferes
/// with by design. Draw the thread with thread.femaleRelief(d, P, m, 0) and
/// prove the fit with thread.engaged. Place with remap().
nut(nominalDia) {
  af = catalog.nutAcrossFlats(nominalDia);
  m = catalog.nutHeight(nominalDia);
  // Face chamfers shave only the hex corners: 45° cones through radius
  // 0.52·af at each face, so the flats (0.5·af) keep their full height
  // and the corners (0.577·af) get the standard circular chamfer.
  body = max(drill.hexXY(af), -p.z, p.z - m,
             ((length(p.xy) - 0.52 * af) + (p.z - m)) * 0.7071,
             ((length(p.xy) - 0.52 * af) + (-p.z)) * 0.7071);
  bore = length(p.xy) - thread.minorDia(nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia)) / 2.0;
  corner = af * 0.57735027;
  form = max(body, -bore);
  form.box = [[-af / 2.0, -corner, 0.0], [af / 2.0, corner, m]];
  form.simple = nutStand(nominalDia);
  return form;
}

/// ISO 4032 hex nut with a display-only helical thread: nut() with the ISO 68-1
/// internal form cut into its bore, the counterpart of shcsThreaded. Prove
/// against nut().
nutThreaded(nominalDia) {
  form = max(nut(nominalDia), neg(thread.female(nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia))));
  form.color = plainSteel();
  stand = nutStand(nominalDia);
  stand.color = femalePaint(nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia), catalog.nutHeight(nominalDia), 0.0, threadIso);
  form.simple = stand;
  return form;
}

/// The hex nut with a SINUSOIDAL thread cut into its bore, the counterpart of
/// shcsThreadedSin.
nutThreadedSin(nominalDia) {
  form = max(nut(nominalDia), neg(thread.femaleSin(nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia))));
  form.color = plainSteel();
  stand = nutStand(nominalDia);
  stand.color = femalePaint(nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia), catalog.nutHeight(nominalDia), 0.0, threadSin);
  form.simple = stand;
  return form;
}

/// ISO 7089 plain washer at catalog nominal, resting on z = 0. Place with
/// remap().
washer(nominalDia) {
  od = catalog.washerOd(nominalDia);
  innerDia = catalog.washerId(nominalDia);
  t = catalog.washerThickness(nominalDia);
  form = max(length(p.xy) - od / 2.0, innerDia / 2.0 - length(p.xy),
             -p.z, p.z - t);
  form.box = [[-od / 2.0, -od / 2.0, 0.0], [od / 2.0, od / 2.0, t]];
  return form;
}

/// ISO 2338 dowel pin: a plain cylinder of the given diameter and length,
/// centered at the origin along Z, with chamfered ends. Dowels are sized
/// by diameter directly (any value is legal, not table-bound).
dowel(diameter, pinLength) {
  c = min(0.15 * diameter, 0.5);
  form = max(length(p.xy) - diameter / 2.0, abs(p.z) - pinLength / 2.0,
             ((length(p.xy) - diameter / 2.0)
              + (abs(p.z) - pinLength / 2.0) + c) * 0.7071);
  form.box = [[-diameter / 2.0, -diameter / 2.0, -pinLength / 2.0],
              [diameter / 2.0, diameter / 2.0, pinLength / 2.0]];
  return form;
}

// =========================================================
// Finishes
// =========================================================
//
// A FINISH IS THE MATERIAL A PART WEARS, and on a threaded part it carries the
// thread. Each builder below is the smooth maximum-material part — the form a
// proof runs against, unchanged — handed back with its material set: the
// finish, with the thread as a displacement height only the renderer reads.
// One call, one body:
//
//   screw = remap(fastener.shcs(6.0, 20.0, steelMat, fastener.threadIso), at);
//
// The material travels with the part, through remap and into whatever it is
// composed with, as a material set on the name would. So does its colour:
// steel, with the thread painted on it (thread.reliefColor) wherever the
// renderer is not drawing the thread as displacement — a part small on screen,
// a mesh, an export. Setting the part's colour after the call replaces it.
//
// The form is threadIso, the ISO 68-1 profile (thread.maleRelief), or
// threadSin, a cosmetic sinusoid with no corners (thread.maleSinRelief) that
// stays smooth where the V's flats would alias, or threadPaint, the ISO
// thread as paint alone (thread.paint) on a finish with no height at all —
// the cheapest of the three, and the one a mesh shows as it is. A last
// argument gives the head a finish of its own: everything above the thread's
// top takes it, the shank's finish everything below.
//
// Each carries its stand-in (see Stand-ins), in the finish without the
// thread's height: the relief painted on it, and paint given up for plain
// steel.

/// The ISO 68-1 thread form, for a finish.
threadIso = 1.0;

/// A cosmetic sinusoidal thread form, for a finish.
threadSin = 2.0;

/// The ISO 68-1 thread as PAINT on the finish, for a finish: no displacement,
/// so nothing the renderer has to march, and the thread a colour everywhere.
threadPaint = 3.0;

/// The material that draws an EXTERNAL thread of `form` on `finish`: the
/// thread between the planes z = `zTop` and z = `zBottom` as a displacement
/// height on a shank at the `major` diameter, or `finish` itself when the
/// thread is paint.
maleFinish(finish, major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom, form) {
  return form > 2.5 ? finish
                    : material.withDisplacement(finish, maleHeight(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom, form));
}

// maleHeight: the external thread of `form` as a displacement height, the ISO
// one for paint.
maleHeight(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom, form) {
  return form > 1.5 && form < 2.5 ? thread.maleSinRelief(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom)
                                  : thread.maleRelief(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom);
}

/// The colour that paints maleFinish's thread on steel where it is not drawn,
/// and everywhere when it is paint.
maleColor(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom, form) {
  steelMat, steelColor = metal.steel();
  return form > 2.5 ? malePaint(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom, form)
                    : thread.reliefColor(steelColor, maleHeight(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom, form), pitchVal);
}

// malePaint: the external thread of `form` painted on steel at full strength.
malePaint(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom, form) {
  return thread.paint(plainSteel(), maleHeight(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom, form), pitchVal);
}

/// The material that draws an INTERNAL thread of `form` on `finish`, on a bore
/// at the basic minor diameter between the planes z = `zTop` and z = `zBottom`,
/// or `finish` itself when the thread is paint.
femaleFinish(finish, major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom, form) {
  return form > 2.5 ? finish
                    : material.withDisplacement(finish, femaleHeight(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom, form));
}

// femaleHeight: the internal thread of `form` as a displacement height, the
// ISO one for paint.
femaleHeight(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom, form) {
  return form > 1.5 && form < 2.5 ? thread.femaleSinRelief(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom)
                                  : thread.femaleRelief(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom);
}

/// The colour that paints femaleFinish's thread on steel where it is not drawn,
/// and everywhere when it is paint.
femaleColor(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom, form) {
  steelMat, steelColor = metal.steel();
  return form > 2.5 ? femalePaint(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom, form)
                    : thread.reliefColor(steelColor, femaleHeight(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom, form), pitchVal);
}

// femalePaint: the internal thread of `form` painted on steel at full strength.
femalePaint(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom, form) {
  return thread.paint(plainSteel(), femaleHeight(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom, form), pitchVal);
}

// plainSteel: steel's colour, metal.steel()'s.
plainSteel() {
  steelMat, steelColor = metal.steel();
  return steelColor;
}

// headed: m below the plane z = zHead, headFinish above it.
headed(m, headFinish, zHead) {
  return material.blend(m, headFinish, p.z > zHead ? 1.0 : 0.0);
}

/// ISO 4762 socket head cap screw, finished: shcs with `finish` and its
/// thread of `form` on the whole shank.
shcs(nominalDia, shankLength, finish, form) {
  s = shcs(nominalDia, shankLength);
  s.material = maleFinish(finish, nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia),
                          0.0, 0.0 - shankLength, form);
  s.color = maleColor(nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia), 0.0, 0.0 - shankLength, form);
  stand = shcsStand(nominalDia, shankLength);
  stand.material = finish;
  stand.color = maleStandColor(nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia), 0.0, 0.0 - shankLength, form);
  s.simple = stand;
  return s;
}

/// The finished cap screw with its head in `headFinish`.
shcs(nominalDia, shankLength, finish, form, headFinish) {
  s = shcs(nominalDia, shankLength);
  s.material = headed(maleFinish(finish, nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia),
                                 0.0, 0.0 - shankLength, form),
                      headFinish, 0.0);
  s.color = maleColor(nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia), 0.0, 0.0 - shankLength, form);
  stand = shcsStand(nominalDia, shankLength);
  stand.material = headed(finish, headFinish, 0.0);
  stand.color = maleStandColor(nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia), 0.0, 0.0 - shankLength, form);
  s.simple = stand;
  return s;
}

/// ISO 10642 countersunk screw, finished: the thread starts under the cone,
/// (dk - d) / 2 below the face, and runs to the tip.
countersunk(nominalDia, overallLength, finish, form) {
  s = countersunk(nominalDia, overallLength);
  s.material = maleFinish(finish, nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia),
                          0.0 - (catalog.cskHeadDia(nominalDia) - nominalDia) / 2.0,
                          0.0 - overallLength, form);
  s.color = maleColor(nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia),
                     0.0 - (catalog.cskHeadDia(nominalDia) - nominalDia) / 2.0,
                     0.0 - overallLength, form);
  stand = countersunkStand(nominalDia, overallLength);
  stand.material = finish;
  stand.color = maleStandColor(nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia),
                               0.0 - (catalog.cskHeadDia(nominalDia) - nominalDia) / 2.0,
                               0.0 - overallLength, form);
  s.simple = stand;
  return s;
}

/// The finished countersunk screw with its head in `headFinish`.
countersunk(nominalDia, overallLength, finish, form, headFinish) {
  zThread = 0.0 - (catalog.cskHeadDia(nominalDia) - nominalDia) / 2.0;
  s = countersunk(nominalDia, overallLength);
  s.material = headed(maleFinish(finish, nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia),
                                 zThread, 0.0 - overallLength, form),
                      headFinish, zThread);
  s.color = maleColor(nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia), zThread, 0.0 - overallLength, form);
  stand = countersunkStand(nominalDia, overallLength);
  stand.material = headed(finish, headFinish, zThread);
  stand.color = maleStandColor(nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia), zThread, 0.0 - overallLength, form);
  s.simple = stand;
  return s;
}

/// ISO 4017 hexagon head screw, finished, threaded its whole length.
hexScrew(nominalDia, shankLength, finish, form) {
  s = hexScrew(nominalDia, shankLength);
  s.material = maleFinish(finish, nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia),
                          0.0, 0.0 - shankLength, form);
  s.color = maleColor(nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia), 0.0, 0.0 - shankLength, form);
  stand = hexStand(nominalDia, shankLength);
  stand.material = finish;
  stand.color = maleStandColor(nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia), 0.0, 0.0 - shankLength, form);
  s.simple = stand;
  return s;
}

/// The finished hexagon head screw with its head in `headFinish`.
hexScrew(nominalDia, shankLength, finish, form, headFinish) {
  s = hexScrew(nominalDia, shankLength);
  s.material = headed(maleFinish(finish, nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia),
                                 0.0, 0.0 - shankLength, form),
                      headFinish, 0.0);
  s.color = maleColor(nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia), 0.0, 0.0 - shankLength, form);
  stand = hexStand(nominalDia, shankLength);
  stand.material = headed(finish, headFinish, 0.0);
  stand.color = maleStandColor(nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia), 0.0, 0.0 - shankLength, form);
  s.simple = stand;
  return s;
}

/// ISO 4032 hex nut, finished: tapped face to face at the basic minor, the
/// thread of `form` on `finish`.
nut(nominalDia, finish, form) {
  s = nut(nominalDia);
  s.material = femaleFinish(finish, nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia),
                            catalog.nutHeight(nominalDia), 0.0, form);
  s.color = femaleColor(nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia), catalog.nutHeight(nominalDia), 0.0, form);
  stand = nutStand(nominalDia);
  stand.material = finish;
  stand.color = femaleStandColor(nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia), catalog.nutHeight(nominalDia), 0.0, form);
  s.simple = stand;
  return s;
}

// =========================================================
// Stand-ins
// =========================================================
//
// EVERY SCREW AND NUT CARRIES ITS SIMPLER MODEL, name.simple, which the viewer
// draws in its place once the part is small on screen (the Detail slider's
// Simple rung): the same envelope as plain mins and maxes of cylinders, cones
// and prisms, with no chamfer, no drive and no thread. The stand-in travels
// with the part, out of the call and through the placement, as its material
// does. Everything but the viewer draws the part.
//
// THE THREAD STEPS DOWN ONE WAY OF DRAWING when the part does:
//
//   geometry   shcsThreaded, nutThreaded      painted on the stand-in
//   relief     a finish of threadIso/threadSin painted on the stand-in, whose
//                                              finish has no height
//   paint      a finish of threadPaint        given up: plain steel
//   none       shcs, nut                      a plain stand-in
//
// and the Textures rung takes every painted thread, a part's or a stand-in's,
// to plain steel (thread.paint's flat look). A colour set on the part after the
// call is its stand-in's too, so it replaces the paint on both.

// standShank: a shank's stand-in, the plain cylinder `dia` across from the
// tip at z = -shankLength up to `zTop`, its end square.
standShank(dia, shankLength, zTop) {
  return max(length(p.xy) - dia / 2.0, -shankLength - p.z, p.z - zTop);
}

// standCylinder: a cylindrical head's stand-in, `dk` across and `k` tall, its
// top edge square.
standCylinder(dk, k) {
  return max(length(p.xy) - dk / 2.0, -p.z, p.z - k);
}

// standHex: a hex head's or a nut body's stand-in, `acrossFlats` wide and `k`
// tall, its corners square.
standHex(acrossFlats, k) {
  return max(drill.hexXY(acrossFlats), -p.z, p.z - k);
}

// shcsStand: a socket head cap screw's stand-in, which a Torx cap screw shares.
shcsStand(nominalDia, shankLength) {
  return min(standCylinder(catalog.shcsHeadDia(nominalDia), nominalDia),
             standShank(nominalDia, shankLength, nominalDia / 2.0));
}

// countersunkStand: a countersunk screw's stand-in. The cone has no edge to
// break, so it is the head itself.
countersunkStand(nominalDia, overallLength) {
  return min(countersunkHead(catalog.cskHeadDia(nominalDia), nominalDia),
             standShank(nominalDia, overallLength, 0.0));
}

// panStand: a pan head screw's stand-in, whatever its recess. The domed head
// is a cylinder and a sphere already, so it is the head itself.
panStand(nominalDia, shankLength) {
  k = catalog.panHeadHeight(nominalDia);
  return min(panHead(catalog.panHeadDia(nominalDia), k, catalog.panCrown(nominalDia)),
             standShank(nominalDia, shankLength, k / 2.0));
}

// hexStand: a hexagon head screw's stand-in.
hexStand(nominalDia, shankLength) {
  k = catalog.hexHeadHeight(nominalDia);
  return min(standHex(catalog.hexHeadAcrossFlats(nominalDia), k),
             standShank(nominalDia, shankLength, k / 2.0));
}

// nutStand: a hex nut's stand-in, bored at the basic minor as the nut is.
nutStand(nominalDia) {
  bore = length(p.xy) - thread.minorDia(nominalDia, thread.metricPitch(nominalDia)) / 2.0;
  return max(standHex(catalog.nutAcrossFlats(nominalDia), catalog.nutHeight(nominalDia)), -bore);
}

// maleStandColor: the colour of a finished screw's stand-in, its external
// thread of `form` painted on, or plain steel when the thread was paint
// already.
maleStandColor(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom, form) {
  return form > 2.5 ? plainSteel() : malePaint(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom, form);
}

// femaleStandColor: the colour of a finished nut's stand-in, as
// maleStandColor is a screw's.
femaleStandColor(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom, form) {
  return form > 2.5 ? plainSteel() : femalePaint(major, pitchVal, zTop, zBottom, form);
}

// =========================================================
// Holes
// =========================================================
//
// ONE KIND OF HOLE. A screw's hole is a drill.hole: the stack the screw's own
// stack reads off, in the SCREW'S frame — mouth on the seating plane, cut down
// −Z — so the seatedAt that places the screw places its hole, and a part
// subtracts the hole meant for it:
//
//   at = fastener.seatedAt(seat, shankDir);
//   screw = remap(fastener.shcs(6.0, 25.0), at);
//   lid = max(lidBody, -remap(fastener.shcsHole(6.0, fastener.fitMedium), at));
//   base = max(baseBody, -remap(fastener.tappedHole(6.0, 25.0), at));
//
// A screw runs through several parts and a hole belongs to one, so each part
// takes its own: the one the head sits in, clearanceHole for every part the
// shank only passes, tappedHole for the part the thread holds. None of them is
// told where its part's faces are, because none needs to: like every drill
// void they are open above and a thru is unbounded, so the part's own faces are
// what bound them — which is also what keeps any surface of the hole off them.
//
// A HEAD ON THE FACE — pan, button, hex — has nothing cut for it, so its part
// takes clearanceHole; a part standing proud of that seat would meet the head,
// and a certify of max(screw, part) says so. A cap screw's counterbore and a
// countersunk screw's countersink are part of their holes, below.
//
// The fit is ISO 273's series: fitFine, fitMedium or fitCoarse.

/// ISO 273 fine series: the tightest clearance, H12.
fitFine = 1.0;

/// ISO 273 medium series, the general-purpose hole, H13.
fitMedium = 2.0;

/// ISO 273 coarse series, H14.
fitCoarse = 3.0;

/// The hole a screw of this size passes through, at ISO 273 `fit`: a thru at
/// the clearance diameter, for a part the shank only crosses or a head sits on.
/// A void in the screw's frame — remap it with the screw's own seatedAt and
/// subtract it.
clearanceHole(nominalDia, fit) {
  clear = catalog.clearance(nominalDia, fit);
  return drill.hole(drill.thru(clear), 0.0);
}

/// The clearance stopped `depth` below the seat, flat-bottomed and open above:
/// for a part the screw clears for that far and then threads into, the tapped
/// part and the clamped one being the same solid. Union tappedHole under it
/// for the thread's own hole.
clearanceHole(nominalDia, fit, depth) {
  clear = catalog.clearance(nominalDia, fit);
  return drill.hole(drill.bore(clear, depth), 0.0);
}

/// The hole an ISO 4762 cap screw's head sits down in: the DIN 974-1
/// counterbore, its floor on the seating plane and open above, over the ISO 273
/// clearance at `fit`. For shcs, shcsTorx and their threaded forms.
shcsHole(nominalDia, fit) {
  bored = catalog.counterbore(nominalDia);
  clear = catalog.clearance(nominalDia, fit);
  return drill.hole([drill.bore(bored, 0.0), drill.thru(clear)], 0.0);
}

/// The hole an ISO 10642 countersunk screw seats flush in: a 90-degree
/// countersink whose rim is the head's theoretical sharp diameter on the
/// seating plane — the maximum-material head, so the two cones coincide — over
/// the ISO 273 clearance at `fit`.
countersunkHole(nominalDia, fit) {
  rim = catalog.cskHeadDia(nominalDia);
  clear = catalog.clearance(nominalDia, fit);
  return drill.hole([drill.countersink(rim, 1.5707963), drill.thru(clear)], 0.0);
}

/// The hole the thread of a screw `shankLength` long holds in: drilled at the
/// basic MINOR diameter — the tapped hole at maximum material, the same bore
/// thread.female cuts its flanks from — to three pitches past the screw's tip,
/// with a 118-degree drill point. Three pitches is the shop allowance for the
/// drill's point and the tap's lead, not a standard's number. Display the
/// thread on the part with thread.femaleRelief.
tappedHole(nominalDia, shankLength) {
  pitchVal = thread.metricPitch(nominalDia);
  minor = thread.minorDia(nominalDia, pitchVal);
  depth = shankLength + 3.0 * pitchVal;
  return drill.hole(drill.blind(minor, depth), 0.0);
}
